text:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. **Primary Request and Intent:**

   Continuation of **VelaPaw** — an on-device-AI multi-pet smart feeder (contest 2026, team contest2026_043_CircuitForge, GitHub login KingMbewe). Project lives at **D:\openVela** (NOT C:). All work this session is 3D CAD in OpenSCAD on the single file `D:\openVela\contest2026_043_CircuitForge\hardware\velapaw_feeder.scad`.

   The session opened by completing a carried-over answer to "is every part printable", where I distinguished *geometrically valid* (verified) from *print-ready* (not yet), and offered to add print-orientation variants.

   The user then made three requests:
   - **"yes"** — approving the offer to add print-orientation variants to the `part` dropdown so each part exports already laid down correctly. **(COMPLETE)**
   - **[Pasted JLCPCB 3D-printing design rules]** — implied request to audit the model against them (min wall by technology, 45° overhang rule, mating clearances, min hole ⌀0.5, pillar rule, text minimums, watertight checklist). **(COMPLETE)**
   - **"Lets go with the mjf/sls as you suggested"** — the process decision, which triggers an MJF/SLS-specific clearance retune. **(IN PROGRESS — analysis done, no edits made yet)**

   **STANDING CONSTRAINTS THAT MUST PERSIST (verbatim intent):**
   - **"log no push"**: export AI-coding logs into `D:\openVela\contest2026_043_CircuitForge\logs\KingMbewe\` — **write files ONLY, NEVER git commit/push**.
   - Board-file swaps must preserve the LCD/camera code.
   - Raw `spi_flash` at offset **0x818000 WEDGES the SoC**.
   - **Never enable CONFIG_RTC** (kills the USB console).
   - Console is on USB-Serial/JTAG; UART0 pins (GPIO43/44) can be stolen for GPIO. Stepper IN4 must be GPIO43 (not 44).

2. **Key Technical Concepts:**
   - **OpenSCAD** parametric CAD; `part` Customizer dropdown; CLI `-D` overrides; `--camera` takes **6 values** (eye+center) or 7 (gimbal) — **9 values fails with exit code 2**; `--viewall --autocenter` is easier; `--colorscheme=Tomorrow`.
   - **`include <>` vs `use <>`**: `use` imports modules/functions only (no top-level geometry, no globals) — probe scripts must pass literals (e.g. `stand_support(-2)` not `stand_support(SUPP_Y1)`, which would be `undef`).
   - **CGAL validation**: `Simple: yes` + `Volumes: N` where N−1 = connected solids. **`Volumes: 2` also proves no internal voids** — relevant to MJF trapped-powder checks.
   - **What CGAL will NOT catch**: two solids that volumetrically overlap in a union just merge; coincident faces merge into one volume. Needs `intersection()` probes + connected-component counting.
   - **Print-orientation vs assembly-orientation**: all parts are modelled in assembly coordinates; the stand parts sit 122–127mm below Z=0, which some slicers refuse to load.
   - **Overhang convention**: a face at 30° from horizontal = 60° from vertical = past the 45° threshold = needs support.
   - **MJF/SLS characteristics**: powder bed → no supports at all; 1.0mm min wall; 0.6mm mating clearance; ±0.3mm tolerance (better than FDM's ±0.5mm); PA12 nylon flexes rather than snapping; JLC nests/orients parts themselves so STL orientation is advisory; blind holes need de-powdering access.
   - **Contest log tool** crashes on a cp1252 console printing its ✅ prefix — needs `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8`.

3. **Files and Code Sections:**

   **`D:\openVela\contest2026_043_CircuitForge\hardware\velapaw_feeder.scad`** — the sole file edited (~1265 lines after edits).

   - **Header PRINTING section (~line 25) — REWRITTEN.** It was stale (still said to print the whole 268mm stand, claimed board_cradle_stand needed no supports). Now:
     ```
     // PRINTING (PLA or PETG, 0.2mm layers, 3 perimeters)
     // 12 parts, 13 pieces -- the A-frame support is printed TWICE. Leave PRINT_POSE
     // on and each one exports already rotated for the slicer and sitting on z=0; the
     // PRINT POSES block at the bottom of the file has the measured bed sizes and the
     // reasoning behind each orientation. Nothing here exceeds 220 x 220 x 250.
     //
     //   FEEDER MECHANISM
     //   part="rotor"         - flat, hub up. No supports.
     //   part="housing"       - bore vertical. No supports (34mm bridges ...).
     //   part="plate_stepper" - flat. No supports. ...
     //   part="plate_idle"    - flat, boss up. No supports.
     //   part="hopper"        - MOUTH DOWN. No supports. It is FLIPPED from the way
     //                          it is modelled: the drum saddle eats the whole neck
     //                          face, so neck-down there is literally nothing to
     //                          start on. See PRINT POSES.
     //
     //   BOARD MOUNT (print one or the other, not both -- the stand contains the
     //   cradle). Holds the ESP32-S3 screen-UP with the camera looking down.
     //   part="board_cradle"  - flat, clamps up. No supports (45deg lead-in ramps).
     //   part="board_cradle_stand" - ... SUPPORTS under the tray. THIS is the mount
     //                          the finished appliance uses ...
     //
     //   STAND -- five printed pieces that bolt together. part="stand" is a PREVIEW
     //   of them assembled and is 164 x 268 x 172: do not try to print it.
     //   part="base_front"    - floor down. No supports. Bowl end of the base plate.
     //   part="base_back"     - floor down. No supports. Column end. Bolts to
     //                          base_front at BASE_SEAM_Y with 2x M3x22 socket cap.
     //   part="support"       - PRINT TWO. Lies flat on its face, 90 x 167 x 16.
     //                          No supports.
     //   part="chute"         - pads down. SUPPORTS under the trough.
     //   part="pedestal"      - posts up. SUPPORTS under the rails and tie bar.
     //   The three towers then bolt DOWN through the base plate, 2x M3x12 countersunk
     //   per foot -- 6 feet, 12 screws. Join the two base tiles FIRST: the splice
     //   bosses sit inside the tray and a fitted tower is in the way.
     //
     //   part="all"           - preview of the assembly (do NOT print this).
     ```

   - **`PRINT_POSE` customizer (after the `part` dropdown, ~line 54) — ADDED:**
     ```scad
     // export printable parts already rotated into their slicing orientation and
     // sitting on z=0. Turn OFF to see a part where it lives in the machine instead.
     // See the PRINT POSES block at the bottom of the file. Previews always ignore it.
     PRINT_POSE = true;   // [true, false]
     ```

   - **`SUPP_FOOT_DY` (~line 855) — ADDED**, with `FOOT_SUP` updated:
     ```scad
     // Resulting Y ranges, which is what has to be checked whenever any of these
     // move:  ped pad -76..-50 | splice bosses -47..-17 | front pad -6..10 |
     //        chute pad 13..29 | back pad 37..53.  Smallest gap 3mm (front pad to
     //        chute pad); the back pad ends 7mm short of the plate's back edge.
     SUPP_FOOT_L  = 16;     // A-frame pad depth (Y); the bar itself is 8
     SUPP_FOOT_X  = 34;     // A-frame screw spread from centre (X)
     // The pad is NOT centred on the frame, and that is a print decision, not a
     // structural one. The frame is SAD_T thick and centred on yc; a pad centred on
     // yc too would stand 4mm proud on BOTH faces, so laying the A-frame down to
     // print it would leave the frame floating 4mm off the bed -- and upright it is
     // a 167mm tower on a 16mm-deep footprint. Offsetting the pad by SAD_T/2 makes
     // its -Y face coplanar with the frame's, so the whole part lies flat on that
     // one plane (see the PRINT POSES block near the bottom). Nothing else cares:
     // the pad lives inside the plate's tray pocket, which is already open there, so
     // the only thing that moves with it is its own pair of screw holes.
     SUPP_FOOT_DY = SAD_T / 2;
     CHUTE_LEG_Y  = 17;     // chute leg front edge
     CHUTE_LEG_L  = 8;      // chute leg depth (Y)
     FOOT_SUP     = [ for (yc = [SUPP_Y1, SUPP_Y2])
                          [0, yc + SUPP_FOOT_DY, SUPP_W, SUPP_FOOT_L, SUPP_FOOT_X, 0] ];
     FOOT_CHU     = [ for (sx = [-16, 16]) [sx, CHUTE_LEG_Y + CHUTE_LEG_L/2, 14, 16, 0, 5] ];
     FOOT_PED     = (LAYOUT == "front") ? [] : [ for (sx = [-48, 48]) [sx, -63, 14, 26, 0, 9] ];
     FEET_ALL     = concat(FOOT_SUP, FOOT_CHU, FOOT_PED);
     ```

   - **`FOOT_T` (~line 803) — CHANGED 8 → 9** (the JLC audit's one real violation):
     ```scad
     FOOT_T       = 9;      // flange thickness. Deep enough for 7mm of M3 thread.
                            // Two screws per foot, so the screws themselves stop it
                            // rotating -- no separate dowel or spigot is needed.
                            // (Footprint and screw spread are per-tower; see FEET_ALL.)
                            // 9 not 8: the pilot has to stay 7 deep (an M3x12 through
                            // a 5mm floor protrudes exactly 7, and a shallower hole
                            // would bottom the screw out before it pulled tight), so
                            // at FOOT_T=8 the blind end was capped by just 1.1mm --
                            // under JLCPCB's 1.2mm FDM minimum wall, and the sort of
                            // thing their DFM check flags. 9 caps it at 2.0mm. The
                            // extra millimetre is free: the flange only has to clear
                            // the plate's 6mm rim, and the nearest thing above any of
                            // the six is the chute floor, 70mm up.
     FOOT_FIT     = 0.3;    // per-side clearance of the plate's relief pocket
     FOOT_SCREW   = 3.4;    // M3 clearance through the plate
     FOOT_PILOT   = 2.5;    // M3 self-tap pilot in the flange
     FOOT_CSK     = 6.4;    // 90deg countersink major dia under the plate
     ```

   - **`tower_foot` pilot (~line 873) — CHANGED:**
     ```scad
     module tower_foot(f) {
         difference() {
             translate([f[0] - f[2]/2, f[1] - f[3]/2, STAND_BASE_Z]) cube([f[2], f[3], FOOT_T]);
             for (s = [-1, 1])
                 translate([f[0] + s*f[4], f[1] + s*f[5], STAND_BASE_Z - 0.1])
                     // The hole starts 0.1 BELOW the flange (an epsilon, so the two
                     // faces are not coplanar), so it needs 7 + 0.1 of length to end
                     // up exactly 7 deep measured from the seating face -- which is
                     // what an M3x12 through a 5mm floor actually needs. Cap = 2.0.
                     cylinder(d = FOOT_PILOT, h = (FOOT_T - 2) + 0.1, $fn = 24);
